what is that professor  it is a historic artifact so i was doing  a bit of clearing out the other day and  i found this this is from 1989 it is  what used to be called a pre-print so  when you wrote a paper it took a while  for it to actually appear in print  submit the paper it will get referees so  it would get looked at it will get  assessed and then it will be accepted  for publication but the time between a  paper being accepted and getting printed  back then was you know maybe six months  and so what you do is once a paper was  accepted you produce one of these things  called free print  and your institutions are at that point  i was at the harvard smithsonian center  for astrophysics they had a mailing list  that they'd send copies of these out to  other astronomical institutions around  the world and also  once a paper was accepted for  publication people would get to know  that the paper existed and you'd  actually people would send you postcards  saying please can you send me a copy of  this paper and you send them one of  these a pre-print of it so this is the  thing that now there's this electronic  thing called archive which essentially  does the same thing that allows you to  disseminate your results before they get  you know appear imprinted in uh in a  journal but this was this was kind of  the pre-archive version of what what a  uh pre-print looked like do you remember  whether or not you got many postcards i  do remember receiving some because i  remember being surprised when the first  one showed up in the post um but i don't  remember there being huge demand for  this paper honestly and what is it about  can you can you tell us about some  research it's called the distribution of  cluster members in the vicinity of a  central dominant galaxy so if you've got  a cluster of galaxies you often find  there's a very big bright galaxy in the  middle of it this thing called a central  dominant galaxy and then when you look  in more detail at those central dominant  galaxies you often find there are sort  of secondary knots condensations of  light within them either these are kind  of physically associated with that  central dominant galaxy and probably  there are other galaxies that are kind  of in the process of being cannibalized  by their kind of partially digested  galaxies that happen to crash into it  and are kind of merging with it or the  alternative is that they're just  galaxies that just happen to lie along  the same line of sight when they get  just in front of it or behind it and so  the point of this paper the motivation  for it was to try and figure out which  of those things was happening  and the way we did that was  essentially just to count the number of  galaxies as you go away from the center  to see how the number drops off as you  go away further and further away because  if you know if you know how that  number's dropping off you can kind of  extrapolate as to what should be  happening right in the middle and hence  how many things should just be there by  chance anyway essentially we ended up  averaging a whole bunch of clusters  together so there's not enough  information if you look at one cluster  there's maybe 20 or 30 galaxies it's  very hard from that very noisy  information to figure out how the  distribution goes with radius so what we  ended up doing was taking a dozen or so  clusters and kind of stacking the data  up together to see what the average  profile was i should say the answer by  the way was it's about if you want to  like say how many of those multiple  nuclei are just kind of the  extrapolation of that profile into the  center it's about half of them so it  looks like a good fraction of them  really are just kind of chance line of  sight things but but there is still an  over density of them that seem to be  physically associated with that central  galaxy i was phd student steve kent was  my phd supervisor so this was the first  piece of work that we did together who  should be getting the glory here i don't  i don't know where to apportion the  credit steve definitely so it was his  data the data already existed not that  long before this when people took  pictures they used photographic plates  and this was ccd data so this was using  modern detectors  like you know the clients in modern  video cameras and it was quite  innovative to use ccd data at that point  so i was using kind of the you know the  cutting edge data that was available at  the time but steve had actually taken it  he made the observations and he kind of  said well here's a load of data you can  play around with so it was kind of the  first project he gave me to play with  what's it like when you have your first  paper accepted for a publication it's  very exciting yeah i was yes and  actually the first time i saw a little  bit before the paper was accepted i gave  my first seminar about it i gave a talk  about it which was just an internal talk  at the center for astrophysics but the  center for astrophysics was kind of a  scary place in that there are you know  250 phd astronomers including some you  know really big names in astronomy so i  was a bit terrified given my first ever  talk about my own research but i gave  this talk and afterwards steve came up  to me afterwards and said take the rest  of the day off so i know i've done all  right or he was sheltered  when you read it now i mean has this  research been superseded does it still  hold water would you do things  differently i mean there's loads more  data by a complete coincidence i have a  phd student at the moment that i'm  supervising who  as kind of a side project to her main  project he's essentially doing this  research again but with much better data  because now there is you know there are  images of large chunks of the sky there  are these huge surveys that you can just  tap into and get the data from she she  will actually hopefully reproduce the  whole show that i was wrong one or the  other  there's something mildly alarming about  the red color of the cover was that  standard practice or just a choice by  harvard smithsonian i think it was just  them every every different institution  had had its own covers that it had made  so after i was here i was an  organization called sita the canadian  institute for theoretical astrophysics  and they actually had cardboard covers  with a little cutout and so you had to  make sure that your title and name  appeared in exactly the right place on  the front page of the of the paper so  that it would show through the little  cutout in the in the cover but yeah it  was one of those things each institution  had its own covers you said you stumbled  over it while you were doing a clear out  did you  sit back on the sofa and read the whole  thing again or i did yeah  i wrote quite well even then  [Laughter]  did you find any typos i know  it stands up quite well it was quite  well written what helped was so this was  the first paper you go through this  process if you write a paper you submit  it to a journal and then it gets sent  out to a referee who actually assesses  it and comes back with their you know  whether it's publishable or not whether  there's any changes you want to make so  this is my first paper and i had a  really nice referee who actually sent  back an annotated copy of your  manuscript with all my typos and things  indicated on it so i actually had that  to help me make sure that the version  that finally got printed and the version  that ended up in the preprint hopefully  had all the typos taken out i always  thought writing these papers you were  supposed to be quite dry and there was  almost like a template to follow can you  express any  writing ability in a scientific paper i  think so and you have to right because  you want it to be readable you know  you're right it has to you know there  are certain conventions there are  certain ways that you say things but  nonetheless you want it to be  sufficiently kind of engaging that the  person reading it doesn't give up and  actually keeps reading it and actually  you know you want to sell your science  right you actually want to convince  somebody your results are right  and that what you've done is interesting  and you you can only do that if you  write reasonably well because if you  write really badly no one's going to  understand what you're trying to say and  they're not going to kind of feel any  particular need to read to the end of it  so it is you know it's part of the  process and actually it's sort of my  favorite part of science is writing  papers i really enjoy that that kind of  skill of writing papers can good or even  great discoveries be lost to science  because they were poorly written up i  guess we'll never know  unless there was a really good one that  was undiscovered for 20 years and then  someone was like oh my goodness this  person discovered this they just wrote  it poorly but that's probably true and  you know and it it is also true that  different scientists you know as i say i  really enjoy that part of the process  quite a lot of my colleagues view it as  a bit of a chore they'd rather be doing  the science i actually really like  writing about the science explaining the  science so in that sense i'm quite a  useful collaborator for some of my  colleagues because some of them are much  more interested in doing the you know  doing the actual research and the  analysis and then they'll leave it up to  me to actually you know turn it into  into the english that will explain it  but it's the fun part as far as i'm  concerned  that's grand design spiral structure  here we have the other extreme which is  this stuff called flocculant spiral  structure you know you can't pick out  two arms here there's just lots of  little tiny bits of spiral structure and  here we think there might be a different  piece of physics as to what's going on  so in this case we've got the center of  o